<!--
    Backtalk's vibrations. Every sound has its own vibration, which plays even when sound
    feedback is off, so each action can be told apart by touch alone. No two actions share a
    vibration in any of the three forms below.

    See utils/output/HapticPatternParser.java for the format. Each array has three forms:
      1. On and off times in ms, for any vibrator. Actions differ by rhythm alone.
      2. After -9998: pairs of ms and amplitude (0-255), for vibrators that can vary strength.
      3. After -9999: primitive, scale (0-255), delay in ms, for VibrationEffect.Composition.
         Only CLICK (1), QUICK_RISE (4), SLOW_RISE (5), QUICK_FALL (6) and TICK (7) are used,
         because a device that lacks one primitive falls back for the whole pattern, and Pixel
         A-series phones lack THUD and SPIN.

    Vocabulary: taps (TICK) are light, clicks (CLICK) are firm, rises and falls are
    swells. Frequent actions such as focus are one short hit.
-->
<resources>

    <!-- Focus on an item: one soft tap. -->
    <integer-array name="view_hovered_pattern">
        <item>0</item>
        <item>15</item>
        <item>-9998</item><!-- AMPLITUDES: ms, amplitude -->
        <item>12</item>
        <item>110</item>
        <item>-9999</item><!-- SENTINEL SEPARATOR -->
        <item>7</item><!-- TICK -->
        <item>140</item><!-- scale -->
        <item>0</item><!-- delayMs -->
    </integer-array>

    <!-- Focus on an actionable item: one firm click. -->
    <integer-array name="view_actionable_pattern">
        <item>0</item>
